{"number": 93, "text": "               Exercises\n\n\n       43.1    Rewrite these sentences using the words in brackets so that they keep the same meaning.\n                 I’m going to register for a course in statistics next year. (SIGN)\n\n                 Several students did not complete the Moral Philosophy course. (DROP)\n                 Our course finishes on 20 June. (BREAK)\n                 He was forced to leave university after one term. He’d done no work at all. (THROW)\n                 | can’t come out tonight. | have to have my essay finished for tomorrow. (WRITE)\n                 My next term at college starts on 12 September. (Go)\n       43.2    Which of these would make  most students happy  and why?\n                breaking up    dropping out     being thrown out\n                scraping through    mugging  up    swotting up\n\n       43.3    Choose the best phrasal verb from the opposite page to complete this email.\n        |                                                                 _\n                                                                                      Reply     Forward\n\n\n          Dear Auntie Megan,\n            At last my first year exams are over. It's such a relief. | feel as if I've done nothing but\n                           (1) for them for ages. Although I'd             (2) with work quite well\n          during the year, | still needed to             (3) everything that we had covered, of course.\n          Fortunately, everything that | hoped would               (4) in the exam paper did. So | hope\n          I've done OK  and haven't just                (5). Now all | have to do is             (6)\n          one  course assignment, which  | need to hand in by the end of term.\n            We  don't                 (7) till the end of the month and so | won't be home till then. We\n          don't                 (8) until the end of September, so it'll be a lovely long break. | look forward\n          to seeing you soon.\n          Love,\n          Kelly\n\n\n       43.4    Correct the ten phrasal verb mistakes in this paragraph. Either the wrong particles or the\n               wrong  verbs have been used.\n               Alfie hardly worked up at all for his exams. He brushed over on the history of the French Revolution,\n               but no questions on the French Revolution got up in the exam. He was afraid that he would be\n               thrown off university for failing his exams. However, he did just manage to scratch through them and\n               so he will be in college when we return back next term. He has promised to try to keep through with\n               work next year as he is planning to sign in for a couple of quite difficult courses, including business\n               studies.    have to polish over his French because the business studies course involves spending a\n               term in France working in a business. He thinks he can just lift up the language when he gets there,\n               but I think he should study it before he goes because he only has school French.\n\n\n                  Over    to\n                  Go to the Mini dictionary at the end of this book and find more phrasal verbs with break, Is there a\n                  common  theme in their meaning? If so, what Is it?\n\n\n                                                             English Phrasal Verbs in Use Intermediate   91", "char_count": 3384, "mean_conf": 91.43, "word_count": 494, "words": [{"text": "Exercises", "x0": 318, "y0": 95, "x1": 682, "y1": 166, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "43.1", "x0": 142, "y0": 312, "x1": 245, "y1": 355, "conf": 90, "bold": true}, {"text": "Rewrite", "x0": 319, "y0": 310, "x1": 481, "y1": 348, "conf": 87, "bold": false}, {"text": "these", "x0": 493, "y0": 312, "x1": 608, "y1": 348, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "sentences", "x0": 621, "y0": 318, "x1": 830, "y1": 352, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "us